-- title:
--   Residual-width bound for each relative Gaussian atom
-- statement:
--   For observations $x_1,\ldots,x_n\in\mathbb R^d$ and reference coordinates $v_{0i}$, define $a_i(\theta)=e^{\langle x_i,\theta\rangle-\|\theta\|^2/2}/v_{0i}-1$. Let $T_L(\theta)_i=e^{-\|\theta\|^2/2}\sum_{\ell=0}^L\langle x_i,\theta\rangle^\ell/(\ell!v_{0i})-1$, and let $V_L$ be the Gaussian Taylor subspace generated by the constant vector and the scaled coefficient vectors of the degree-$L$ Taylor polynomial. Assume $T,S\ge0$, $\|x_i\|\le T$, $\|\theta\|\le S$, and $v_{0i}\ge e^{-(TS+S^2/2)}$ for every $i$. Put $\varepsilon_L=e^{2TS+S^2/2}(TS)^{L+1}/(L+1)!$. Then $$\|a(\theta)-P_{V_L}a(\theta)\|_2\le\sqrt n\,\varepsilon_L.$$ Thus the finite-dimensional Taylor subspace approximates each admissible atom with an explicit uniform residual.
-- source:
